The player's own part of the map (Phase 40 of the Copilot / Shattered-Maps plan; docs/shattered-maps/surfaces.md "Your own rooms"). A PRODUCTION feature: nothing here imports from features/copilot; a local build adds a history source from the other side (features/copilot/mapHistory.ts registers it).

layerStore.ts keeps one map file per world in IndexedDB (key layer|<world>); layeredApi.ts wraps a WorldMapApi so the layer is merged over the published areas, which is why the session and the window needed no change (sharedWorldMapApi in useWorldMap.ts wraps every client; a layer change makes useWorldMap forget its last area and look again). trail.ts + trailRecorder.ts record titles, exit sets and where each exit led (nothing else) from game events, bound once in pages/MainContainer.tsx; shared.ts is the page's one KvStore (IndexedDB, else memory). history.ts is the registry of history sources: the trail registers as a FALLBACK so it is asked after any source a local build adds, whenever each was added. draft.ts is the logic of components/AddRoomPanel.tsx.

Why this shape: the owner asked for durable IndexedDB storage (memory write-through; no localStorage, a trail record is read once per title and the layer once per world) and, later, a lean id list synced to the C# service (Phase 44, not built). Tests that need a store use memoryKvStore() from autoleveling-idb.ts; sharedMineKv() is memory in jest (no indexedDB).

Tests of this folder sit beside each file (*.test.ts); the window-level behaviour (the Add this room button, fog drawing, the eye) is in components/WorldMapWindow.test.tsx and components/AddRoomPanel.test.tsx, which pass their own stores instead of the shared IndexedDB ones.
